    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2024214208171430136",
      "url": "https://x.com/jason61987/status/2024214208171430136",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/jason61987/status/2024214208171430136",
      "text": "On Wednesday, February 18, 2026: A very frgid double barrel 528 DM low axis approximately 185 miles off the coast of Oregon and Washington state that arrived from the Gulf of Alaska 36 hours prior and before that came from the origins of the Siberian Peninsula two weeks ago.  \n\nThe continued warming of the Pacific from the Philippines to California has significantly energized these intensifications of all these storms into powerful dynamic surface cold fronts that have had significant convective parameters bringing midwestern style squall lines from Northern California and  Central California escalating intensity into dangerous weather for Southern California. \n\nThese particular cold fronts have been producing 1.3 to 1.8 inches of rainfall per hour. Especially this past Monday and last night from Tuesday 11 PM to Wednesday 4AM. Each system has been influenced by a powerful polar jet stream of 180 knots slamming into Central and Southern California. \n\nThere’s another very cold storm tonight in this trifecta that's taking shape. That will bring more heavy rain, flooding concerns, damaging winds, and the possibility of a moderate to strong third squall line tomorrow 5 AM - 2PM from San Luis Obispo to San Diego counties. Rainfall amount 0.85 to 1.5 inches additional precipitation.\n\nLast night Santa Maria, Santa Barbara and Rancho Palace Verdes and Long Beach, had an incredibly intense lightning show associated with the second squall line a true Testament of how powerful these storms really have been. Snow levels will continue to drop between 2,800 and 3,000 feet by Thursday 11AM. \n\nThe intensity of snow has been unbelievable up in Truckee California and Donner Pass has received more than 6 feet of snow in the last 24 hours with winds over 80MPH at the ridge tops. Unfortunately, has led to casualties, as skiers were buried by an avalanche during the height of the storm. \n\nThis escalating storm pattern will continue next week with potentially even heavier rain trending next Thursday and Friday, but this storm, which is coming back again from the Hawaiian islands is being looked at very closely in the next 24- 36 hours. The latest CPC indicates well above normal rainfall for the last week of February 2026, showing high confidence at this point. #CAwx @realwxforecast @LaInaMinute @RickDickertWX @edgarrmcgregor @BPW_SERVICES714 @SnowPalsDotOrg @RobMayeda @SoCalweathernet",
